# Weekly /retro (fleet-ops#9314). One scheduled run a week, Monday 07:00 IST:
# pi reads one week of fleet sessions, writes the retro report, and files one
# needs-orchestrator issue so a person triages the findings. Shape copied from
# blacksmith-flip.service (fleet-ops#8936): one prompt file installed by
# fleet-sync, piped into `pi --print`; no glue, the prompt is the whole job.
#
# gh auth: this unit runs under the systemd --user manager, which has no
# GH_TOKEN, so `gh` reads ~/.config/gh/hosts.yml and acts as the nish3451
# account, which can open a fleet-ops issue. No credential is in this file, in
# the prompt, or in argv. Proved on the live run: issue #9332 was opened at
# 2026-10-06 11:36 IST.
#
# This unit is NOT in the worker jail (agent.yml's empty-tmpfs over
# ~/.config/gh and ~/.config/cloudflare, fleet-ops#9274), and unlike
# blacksmith-flip it does read untrusted text: the week's sessions carry
# outsider issue bodies and PR reviews. So a fooled model here can reach
# nish3451's gh login and the no-expiry Cloudflare token. That is a known,
# named risk, not an oversight, and the bound is the prompt plus the loaded
# ~/AGENTS.md: read-only sources, "do not edit any file except $DELIVERABLE",
# exactly ONE issue with no `agent-ready`, no PRs, no deploy.
# InaccessiblePaths= does not fence this under the --user manager on this
# host (systemd 255.4). A oneshot run 2026-10-06 12:53 IST with
# InaccessiblePaths=/home/nish/.config/cloudflare /home/nish/.config/fleet-ops/litellm-master-key.env
# showed the property set (`systemctl --user show -p InaccessiblePaths`) and
# `systemd-analyze --user verify` accepted the file, but the unit's own
# `test -e` still printed cf=yes and key=yes. Shipping the line would look
# like a fence and not be one. The remaining exposure is the gh login;
# fleet-ops#9339 is the narrowing (an issues-only token plus GH_CONFIG_DIR)
# once Nish mints the token.
#
# Why stepfun/step-5-preview and no --no-context-files: that seat read a full
# week of sessions and wrote the pilot report in 28.4 min on 2026-10-06
# (retro-week-2026-10-06.md, 18,952 bytes), and ~/AGENTS.md must stay loaded —
# it is what makes the report plain English. FLEET_TASK_ID is a fixed id on
# purpose: a recurring check, not a per-task agent (blacksmith-flip comment).
#
# DELIVERABLE carries the date, and the date is computed ONCE. ExecStartPre writes
# today's IST date to ~/workspaces/agent-state/retro/.retro-weekly-date, and
# both ExecStart and ExecStopPost read that file back. Three `date +%%F` calls
# (start, stop, and the grep) would disagree on a Persistent=true catch-up run
# that starts before midnight IST and stops after it: the report is named with
# the start date and the dead-man check would look for the stop date. One
# write makes the start date, the model's $DELIVERABLE and the dead-man check
# the same string. The report filename stays retro-week-<date>.md.
#
# Deadline: the issue text names RuntimeMaxSec=3600, but for Type=oneshot
# systemd ignores RuntimeMaxSec — `systemd-analyze --user verify` prints
# "RuntimeMaxSec= has no effect in combination with Type=oneshot. Ignoring.",
# so shipping it would be dead config. The working deadline knob for a oneshot
# is TimeoutStartSec, which defaults to infinity for oneshot: left alone, a
# wedged model call hangs the unit forever. 3600 s covers the proven 28.4 min
# pilot with headroom, and the kill lands as Result=timeout -> a failed unit
# (`systemctl --user list-units --state=failed`), with ExecStopPost still
# running the deliverable check.
#
# Two gates, one for each thing this run promises. ExecStopPost checks the
# report file, and ExecStart checks that the run actually filed the issue
# (fleet-ops#9327: a litellm 403 killed a run mid-flight and the unit stopped
# with exit 0, because `out=$(...)` swallowed pi's status and the last command
# was a printf). Same shape as blacksmith-flip's FLIP: trap: the prompt's last
# line is `RETRO_ISSUE_URL: https://github.com/Nishfleet/fleet-ops/issues/N`
# (or `RETRO_ISSUE_URL:` on failure) and a check that is absent fails the unit.
# A failure is a failed unit someone can list, not a silent week with no retro.
# `out=$(… | pi …)` discarding pi's exit is intentional: the last-line grep
# is the gate. Do not wrap the substitution in `set -e` or `pipefail`.
#
# ExecStartPre deletes today's report BEFORE anything else, then resolves the
# date into the state file, then checks the prompt:
# - without the delete, a manual run earlier the same day leaves a non-empty
#   file that a later failed run then passes the ExecStopPost check on;
# - without the prompt check, `pi` starts with stdin closed and writes nothing,
#   and the report the prompt file cannot reach stays silently stale.
# The delete runs first so a half-finished pre-step cannot leave a stale file.
# If ExecStartPre fails, ExecStart does not run but ExecStopPost does, and it
# reads the state file, so the date must be written even on the failure path.
#
# After=fleet-litellm-proxy.service because the model call goes through the
# proxy on 127.0.0.1:4000: a Persistent=true catch-up run can start while the
# proxy is still coming up. No Wants=, so the retro never starts a proxy that
# was deliberately stopped. The ExecStart gate above turns a too-early run
# into a loud failure rather than a fake clean week.
#
# Environment=TZ=Asia/Kolkata: one timezone governs the calendar spec, the
# report filename and the model's "today". The timer is Asia/Kolkata, so pin
# TZ to the same zone instead of inheriting the host clock — on a catch-up run
# starting just after 00:00 IST the window the model computes and the date in
# the filename must not be able to disagree. A comment explaining a directive
# goes ABOVE [Unit]: systemd parses every line inside a section as a directive
# and prints "Missing '=', ignoring line." for prose (this was caught here by
# `systemd-analyze verify` while writing it).
#
# WorkingDirectory=/home/nish is the documented default (`systemctl --user show`
# prints `!/home/nish`), written out: pi's project context discovery and the
# report's plain-English rule both assume ~ (the model must load ~/AGENTS.md).
#
# %%F, not %F: systemd expands % specifiers itself, before any shell runs
# (same rule as the %%s in blacksmith-flip.service, ci.yml gate fleet-ops#8382).
[Unit]
Description=Weekly /retro over one week of fleet sessions (Monday 07:00 IST)
After=network-online.target fleet-litellm-proxy.service
Wants=network-online.target

[Service]
Type=oneshot
Environment=FLEET_TASK_ID=retro-weekly
Environment=TZ=Asia/Kolkata
WorkingDirectory=/home/nish
ExecStartPre=/bin/sh -c 'd=$(date +%%F); /bin/rm -f /home/nish/workspaces/agent-state/retro/retro-week-$d.md; mkdir -p /home/nish/workspaces/agent-state/retro; printf %%s "$d" > /home/nish/workspaces/agent-state/retro/.retro-weekly-date; /bin/test -s /home/nish/.local/share/retro-weekly/prompt.md || { echo no-prompt >&2; exit 1; }'
ExecStart=/bin/sh -c 'd=$(cat /home/nish/workspaces/agent-state/retro/.retro-weekly-date); DELIVERABLE=/home/nish/workspaces/agent-state/retro/retro-week-$d.md; export DELIVERABLE; out=$(cat /home/nish/.local/share/retro-weekly/prompt.md | /home/nish/.local/bin/pi --print --provider stepfun --model step-5-preview); printf "%%s\n" "$out"; printf "%%s\n" "$out" | grep -qE "^RETRO_ISSUE_URL: https://github[.]com/Nishfleet/fleet-ops/issues/[0-9]+$" || exit 1'
ExecStopPost=/bin/sh -c 'd=$(cat /home/nish/workspaces/agent-state/retro/.retro-weekly-date); test -s /home/nish/workspaces/agent-state/retro/retro-week-$d.md || { echo no-deliverable >&2; exit 1; }'
TimeoutStartSec=3600
